{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0a913d18d73b844f0169bb447853b88191b3e3b4f28a739744", "created": 1773880441, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Medical Binder, Online Patient Portals, and Second-Opinion Care Coordination\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community centers on a patient-facing workflow for organizing and carrying comprehensive medical documentation in a medical binder/notebook to improve care coordination and decision-making. Key entities describe the medical binder as a structured container for blood test results, imaging results and reports, cancer-specific reports, and insurance materials, organized into folders by test type and arranged by date. The workflow is supported by online patient portals (e.g., MyChart) that enable patients to view, download, and print records for inclusion in the binder. The organized record set is positioned as especially helpful for medical appointments and seeking second opinions, including at specialized medical centers and with experienced surgeons.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The medical binder is the hub entity: a portable, centralized record-keeping system meant to be brought to appointments\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The medical binder/notebook is described as a centralized place to store and manage copies of key medical records (blood tests, imaging results and reports, and specialized reports such as pathology/radiology/genetics), plus administrative items such as insurance forms, with the explicit intent that it be brought to medical appointments to support care coordination and second opinions [Data: Entities (213, 421, 2391, 214, 1515); Relationships (304, 2450, 2449, 2451, 7626,+more)].\\n\\nThe community also includes an explicit “create a medical binder” activity that instructs the patient to assemble records (tests, imaging, reports), add organizational structure (folders/sections), and include a questions/notes section for use during visits, reinforcing the binder’s role as an operational tool rather than a passive archive [Data: Entities (2530, 213, 216, 1521, 227); Relationships (4593, 2449, 567, 2461, 2462,+more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Online patient portals operationalize record access by enabling viewing, downloading, and printing for binder inclusion\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Online patient portals are described as a convenient and recommended mechanism to access test results and health records (including pathology reports), and to download and print those records for personal retention—specifically to add them to the medical binder [Data: Entities (940, 211, 1533, 3726, 213); Relationships (273, 1926, 2450, 7627, 7629,+more)].\\n\\nThis portal-to-binder pipeline is reinforced by relationships linking imaging results and blood test results to portal access and export, indicating that the binder is intended to be populated from official digital sources rather than informal notes alone [Data: Entities (163, 162, 940, 211, 1533); Relationships (577, 7629, 273, 2450, 7627,+more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The record set emphasizes diagnostic evidence: blood tests, imaging results, and cancer-specific reports\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The binder’s contents are repeatedly characterized around core diagnostic documentation—blood test results, imaging results (including written reports), and reports about the patient’s cancer/specific disease—positioning the binder as a consolidated evidence base for ongoing clinical decisions [Data: Entities (162, 163, 2391, 3704, 1516); Relationships (304, 2452, 4232, 7626, 2454,+more)].\\n\\nImaging results are described in detail as outputs from modalities such as CT, MRI, PET/CT, ultrasound, mammogram, and bone scan, and patients are advised to keep copies of both images and accompanying written reports, consistent with the binder’s role in longitudinal review and discussion with providers [Data: Entities (163, 213, 1515, 2394, 940); Relationships (577, 2452, 4232, 2450, 273,+more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Folder-based structure and date-ordering are key governance mechanisms for keeping records usable over time\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community specifies a folder-based organization approach within the binder (physical or digital), with folders/sections used to separate documentation by test type and category, improving retrieval and clarity during care coordination [Data: Entities (216, 459, 460, 461, 462); Relationships (617, 618, 619, 2461, 2458,+more)].\\n\\nA distinct practice—organizing by date—is recommended for placing items within folders chronologically to track the care timeline; this introduces a lightweight but explicit internal “data governance” convention that helps make the compiled record set auditable and clinically interpretable [Data: Entities (1535, 216, 462, 1515, 213); Relationships (2462, 2461, 617, 618, 619,+more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The binder includes an engagement layer (questions and notes) designed to structure patient–clinician communication\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Beyond document storage, the binder is recommended to include a dedicated questions and notes section, indicating that it is meant to support appointment preparation, capture clinician guidance, and document decisions contemporaneously [Data: Entities (1521, 227, 421, 213, 229); Relationships (2449, 339, 340, 4572, 567,+more)].\\n\\nThis design ties practical tools (e.g., a pen stored in a zipper pocket) directly to the workflow of recording questions and notes, emphasizing usability during real-time interactions in clinical settings rather than retrospective record compilation [Data: Entities (228, 229, 1521, 227, 231); Relationships (340, 4572, 4571, 2449, 339,+more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Second opinions and specialized care are explicit use cases enabled by the assembled records\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The binder and the underlying practice of keeping copies of records are described as especially helpful for second opinions, implying that portability and completeness are central requirements for facilitating external review by additional providers [Data: Entities (287, 213, 2530, 1515, 2394); Relationships (4593, 4232, 2451, 2450, 7626,+more)].\\n\\nThe community also links second opinions to seeking care at a specialized medical center and consulting an experienced surgeon, suggesting that the organized record package is meant to reduce friction when escalating to higher-expertise evaluation (notably referenced in a prostate-cancer context for medical centers) [Data: Entities (3419, 287, 4013, 213, 2391); Relationships (6835, 8342, 2451, 4232, 7626,+more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Operational steps (getting copies, making copies, downloading/printing) form a repeatable, patient-executed workflow\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Multiple entities and relationships describe the patient’s concrete actions: getting copies of records, making copies, and downloading/printing from portals, all with the stated objective of assembling a complete personal set for the binder [Data: Entities (2394, 1534, 1533, 3726, 2530); Relationships (4230, 4232, 2451, 2450, 7627,+more)].\\n\\nThis repeated emphasis indicates the community is primarily procedural and educational in nature: it is less about a single static document and more about establishing an ongoing process for collecting and updating a personal medical archive [Data: Entities (2530, 1515, 2391, 211, 213); Relationships (4593, 273, 2450, 2452, 2451,+more)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Practical administrative readiness is embedded (insurance cards, zipper pocket, small calendar) to support visit logistics\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The binder is described as including practical organizational features such as pockets (including an optional zipper pocket) and space for insurance cards, explicitly supporting administrative readiness at appointments [Data: Entities (213, 231, 228, 421, 216); Relationships (4571, 2450, 567, 4593, 2449,+more)].\\n\\nA small calendar is recommended for inclusion (often stored in the zipper pocket), indicating that scheduling and timeline management (appointments/tests/treatments) are considered part of the broader record-organization and care-coordination workflow [Data: Entities (230, 228, 213, 421, 1515); Relationships (4573, 340, 2462, 567, 4593,+more)].\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 2.3,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The community’s impact severity is low because it describes a benign, patient-oriented record-organization workflow that supports care coordination and second opinions rather than harmful, illegal, or disruptive activity.\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null, "reasoning_content": "**Preparing medical binder report**\n\nI'm gathering information about the \"medical binder\" community. I want to explore topics like patient-created binders, online portals, types of records, and second opinions. My goal is to compile 5-10 findings in paragraph form and assess their severity rating, considering their importance. The practice seems benign, so I might rate it a 2.0. I need to ensure legal compliance, focusing on patient self-management without implying malicious intent. I also need to prepare a JSON output with a concise title."}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 2179, "prompt_tokens": 5816, "total_tokens": 7995, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 247, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}